      <div class="msv__content">
        <p>Moving to Spain feels daunting, but it&#x27;s more manageable than you think. This guide walks you through every step: securing a visa, finding housing, opening a bank account, and settling into your new life. By the end, you&#x27;ll have a clear roadmap.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Choose Your Visa (Months -3 to -1 Before Move)</h2>
<p>Your visa is your foundation. Determine which category fits: Are you working (Work, Digital Nomad, Self-Employment)? Retired (Non-Lucrative)? Studying (Student)? This choice affects your timeline, costs, and documentation. Give yourself 3-4 months before your intended move date. Visa processing takes 4-12 weeks, and you&#x27;ll need time to gather documents.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Start Your Visa Application (Months -3 to -2)</h2>
<p>Contact your local Spanish consulate (the one covering your region). Request their specific documentation list—it varies slightly by consulate. Start gathering: passport, birth certificate, employment contract or income proof, police clearance certificate, proof of housing, health insurance information. Get certified translations of all non-English documents (if required by your consulate).</p>
<h2>Step 3: Secure Housing (Months -2 to Move)</h2>
<p>Find accommodation through Idealista, Fotocasa, or Airbnb. Many expats rent first (6-12 months) before buying—smart move, lets you explore neighborhoods. Budget €600-1,000/month for a 1-bed apartment outside major cities, €1,000-1,500+ in Madrid/Barcelona. Get a lease signed well before your move. Some landlords require references or proof of income; provide whatever they ask.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Submit Your Visa Application (Month -2)</h2>
<p>Submit in person (most consulates require this) or by post if allowed. Bring all original documents, copies, and translations. Pay the fee (€100-300). Get a receipt showing submission date. Note the expected approval date (consulate should tell you; typically 4-12 weeks).</p>
<h2>Step 5: Prepare for Departure (Month -1)</h2>
<p>Notify your landlord/employer in your home country of your moving date. Arrange forwarding of mail. Schedule international moving company if bringing belongings (or use courier services for a few boxes). Set up travel: flights, travel insurance. Start learning Spanish basics if you haven&#x27;t already (apps like Duolingo take 15 mins/day). Arrange initial accommodation (hotel, Airbnb) for first 1-2 weeks while you find a permanent place.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Collect Your Visa &amp; Travel (Week -1 to Week 1)</h2>
<p>Visa approved? Collect it from the consulate (or check if mailed). It&#x27;s typically valid 3-6 months for you to enter Spain. Book your flight, arrange travel insurance. You have 3-6 months from visa approval to actually enter Spain. Then you have 30 days to register with Spanish authorities once you arrive.</p>
<h2>Step 7: Register Locally Upon Arrival (Weeks 1-4)</h2>
<p>Within 30 days of arrival, visit the local Oficina de Extranjería (Immigration Office) with your visa, passport, proof of residence (lease), and health insurance. Register your address. They&#x27;ll issue a residency certificate and a foreign identification number (NIE). Don&#x27;t delay—this is legally required and needed for everything else (bank account, contracts, etc.).</p>
<h2>Step 8: Open a Bank Account (Weeks 2-4)</h2>
<p>Visit major banks: BBVA, CaixaBank, Santander. Bring passport, NIE (residency certificate), proof of address (lease or utility bill), and if possible, a reference letter from your home bank. Opening takes 1-2 hours. Most banks offer English-speaking support. You&#x27;ll get a debit card within 1-2 weeks.</p>
<h2>Step 9: Register for Healthcare (Weeks 2-6)</h2>
<p>Visit your local health center (centro de salud) with passport, NIE, and proof of address. Sign up for Spain&#x27;s public system (free/low-cost once registered). Meanwhile, your initial private health insurance covers you. After 1-2 months, you&#x27;ll typically qualify for public healthcare.</p>
<h2>Step 10: Handle Administration (Weeks 2-8)</h2>
<p>Get a Spanish phone number (Movistar, Vodafone, Orange; €10-30/month). Register with local utilities (electricity, water, internet). If self-employed, register with tax authorities. Explore your neighborhood: grocery stores, pharmacies, restaurants, public transport. Introduce yourself to neighbors. Join local expat groups or hobby clubs.</p>
<h2>Timeline Summary</h2>
<p>3-4 months before: Start visa process. 2 months before: Apply for visa, secure housing. 1 month before: Final preparations, collect visa. Week 1: Arrive in Spain. Week 2-4: Register locally, open bank account, enroll in healthcare. Week 8+: Settled, exploring community.</p>
<h2>Costs Breakdown</h2>
<p>Visa: €100-500. Initial flights: €300-1,000. Housing deposit (usually 2 months rent): €1,200-3,000. Initial furniture/household items: €1,000-3,000. Bank account: Free. Healthcare (first month private): €100-300. Total startup: €3,700-8,300. Monthly ongoing: €1,500-2,500 depending on city and lifestyle.</p>
<p>Ready to Take the Leap?</p>
<p>Spain is waiting. Let&#x27;s map out your specific visa pathway, timeline, and next steps. A consultation clarifies everything.</p>
